   "response_text": "Good, only the library source is modified.\n\n## The scoop \ud83d\udc36\n\n**Root cause:** `IntRange._clamp` in `src/click/types.py` was a stub that ignored its arguments \u2014 when the bound was open it just did `return bound` anyway. So clamping never respected `min_open`/`max_open`, landing the value right on the excluded boundary. (Classic YAGNI-gone-wrong: a parameter that's accepted but never actually used.)\n\n**The fix:**\n```python\ndef _clamp(self, bound, dir, open):\n    if not open:\n        return bound\n    # An open bound excludes the bound itself, so clamp to the\n    # nearest integer in the given direction instead.\n    return bound + dir\n```\n\nFor integers the \"nearest valid value\" is simply `bound + dir`, where `dir` is already `1` for a min clamp and `-1` for a max clamp \u2014 the direction info was being passed in all along and just thrown away. Closed bounds keep returning `bound` unchanged, exactly as required.\n\n**Verification:**\n- `IntRange(0, 10, max_open=True, clamp=True)`: `15 \u2192 9`, `10 \u2192 9` \u2705\n- `IntRange(0, 10, min_open=True, clamp=True)`: `-5 \u2192 1`, `0 \u2192 1` \u2705\n- Closed `IntRange(0, 10, clamp=True)`: `15 \u2192 10`, `-5 \u2192 0` (unchanged) \u2705\n- Both-open sanity: `15 \u2192 9` \u2705\n- Existing suite: `59 passed, 1 skipped`; `git status` shows only `src/click/types.py` touched \u2014 no test files harmed.\n\nDONE",
